%S A400966 8,14,20,24,30,34,38,40,46,50,54,56,60,62,64,64,70,74,78,80,84,86,88,
%T A400966 88,94,98,102,104,108,110,112,112,116,118,120,120,126,130,134,136,140,
%U A400966 142,144,144,148,150,152,152,156,158,160,160,162,162,168,172,176
%N A400966 Minimum number of exposed cubic faces of a 4-dimensional polyhypercube formed by n unit tesseracts.
%C A400966 For each n, a(n) is the minimum number of exposed cubic faces (3-faces) among all face-connected configurations of n unit tesseracts (4-cubes) in the standard 4D cubical lattice.
%C A400966 An optimal configuration can be constructed by (1) forming the largest balanced 4D rectangular block, with side lengths differing by at most one, (2) attaching the remaining tesseracts in a partial 3D layer that minimizes exposed 3-faces, and (3) applying the same principle recursively in lower dimensions. The recurrence follows from Agnarsson and Lauria (2013, Eqs. (14) and (16)); its optimality follows from Theorem 6.4.
%C A400966 The term 2*B*Sum_{i=1..d} (1/b_i) calculates the exposed (d-1)-faces of the largest balanced d-dimensional rectangular block of volume B, counting two opposite faces of area B/b_i in each coordinate direction. The term S(d-1,k-B) gives the net increase in exposed (d-1)-faces when the remaining d-cubes are attached.
%H A400966 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A400966/b400966.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400966 G. Agnarsson and K. Lauria, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.6517">Extremal Subgraphs of the d-Dimensional Grid Graph</a>, arXiv:1302.6517 [math.CO], 2013.
%F A400966 a(n) = S(4,n), where S(d,k) is the minimum surface area of k unit d-dimensional cubes.
%F A400966 For d = 2, 3, 4 and k >= 1, let m = floor(k^(1/d)).
%F A400966 For 1 <= i <= d, let b_i = m+1 if k >= m^(d-i)*(m+1)^i, otherwise b_i = m; then B = Product_{i=1..d} b_i.
%F A400966 S(d,k) = 2*B*Sum_{i=1..d} (1/b_i) + S(d-1,k-B), with initial conditions: S(d,0) = 0 for d = 1, 2, 3, 4; S(1,k) = 2 for k >= 1.
%e A400966 For n = 57, the largest balanced 4D block has dimensions 2*3*3*3, containing 54 tesseracts with surface area 162. Attaching the remaining 3 tesseracts increases the surface area by S(3,3) = 2*2*(1/2 + 1 + 1) + S(2,1) = 10 + 4 = 14. Hence a(57) = S(4,57) = 2*(2*3*3*3)*(1/2 + 1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3) + 14 = 162 + 14 = 176.
%o A400966 (Python)
%o A400966 from math import prod
%o A400966 def S(d, k):
%o A400966     if k == 0: return 0
%o A400966     if d == 1: return 2
%o A400966     m = 1
%o A400966     while (m + 1)**d <= k: m += 1
%o A400966     b = [m + int(k >= m**(d-i)*(m+1)**i) for i in range(1, d+1)]
%o A400966     B = prod(b)
%o A400966     return 2*sum(B//x for x in b) + S(d-1, k-B)
%o A400966 print([S(4, n) for n in range(1, 58)])
%Y A400966 Cf. A027709 (2D analog), A193416 (3D analog).
